{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Full Dev Pipeline",
  "description": "A manually triggered master pipeline that runs the full development workflow in sequence: 1) Pull GitHub issues to identify related work, 2) Analyze spec files for context, 3) Create requirements and tasks from the target issue ticket, 4) Implement code changes for those tasks, 5) Run the test suite, 6) Create an issue-linked branch, commit, push, and open a PR. Trigger this hook when you're ready to plan and ship feature work tied to a GitHub issue.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Execute the full development pipeline in order. Complete each step before moving to the next. Report progress after each step.\n\n**Step 1 — GitHub Issue Sync**\nUse the GitHub MCP tool (mcp_github_list_issues) to pull all open issues from the repository (owner: Piashsarker, repo: inec-election-results). List the open issues and identify the target feature issue (not PRs). If multiple feature issues exist, pick the highest-priority one or ask the user.\n\n**Step 2 — Spec Analysis**\nRead the spec files in .kiro/specs/ (requirements.md, design.md, tasks.md). Summarize what the current spec covers and verify how the target issue relates to existing requirements and design decisions.\n\n**Step 3 — Create Requirements & Tasks from Issue Ticket**\nBased on the target GitHub issue description from Step 1:\n1. Analyze the issue ticket description to understand what needs to be built (frontend, backend, or both).\n2. Read the relevant existing code (API endpoints, frontend pages, types, services) to understand the current state and identify gaps.\n3. Create a requirements document at `.kiro/specs/issue-<number>-<slug>/requirements.md` with:\n   - Introduction referencing the GitHub issue\n   - User stories with acceptance criteria for each deliverable\n4. Create a tasks document at `.kiro/specs/issue-<number>-<slug>/tasks.md` with:\n   - Ordered implementation tasks (backend first, then frontend)\n   - Each task should reference which requirement it fulfills\n   - Tasks should be granular enough to implement in a single code change\nReport the requirements and tasks created.\n\n**Step 4 — Implement Code Changes**\nWork through each task from the tasks.md created in Step 3:\n1. For each task, read the relevant existing code first to understand patterns and conventions.\n2. Implement the change following project architectural patterns:\n   - Backend: async-first, FastAPI with Pydantic schemas, SQLAlchemy queries, proper type annotations\n   - Frontend: Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict mode, Tailwind CSS, existing component patterns\n3. After each file change, verify it compiles/lints cleanly.\n4. Mark tasks as complete in tasks.md as you go.\nReport what was implemented for each task.\n\n**Step 5 — Run Tests**\nRun the test suite: `cd backend && python -m pytest tests/ --tb=short -q 2>&1`\nAlso run frontend lint: `cd frontend && npx next lint 2>&1`\nAlso run backend lint: `cd backend && ruff check app/ 2>&1`\nReport results. If tests or lint fail, fix the issues and re-run.\n\n**Step 6 — Push and Create PR**\nIf all previous steps pass:\n1. Check `git status` for uncommitted changes\n2. Use the target GitHub issue number from Step 1\n3. Create branch: `issue/<issue_number>-<short-description>`\n4. Stage changes (exclude .env and secrets): `git add .` then `git restore --staged '*.env*' '.kiro/settings/*'`\n5. Commit: `feat(scope): description (#issue_number)`\n6. Push: `git push -u origin <branch>`\n7. Create PR via GitHub MCP tool targeting main, referencing the issue with a description that lists the requirements addressed and tasks completed\n\nReport the final PR URL when complete."
  }
}